  </div><section class="section pain-section" id="problem">
  <div class="page-shell">
  <div class="pain-heading" data-reveal="">
  <div>
  <p class="eyebrow">The real problem</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">How many reorders are still hiding in email threads?</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-copy">Most company store opportunities do not fail because you cannot supply the products. They lose momentum because ordering becomes too slow, too manual, or too difficult to repeat.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pain-grid">
  <article class="pain-card" data-reveal="">
  <span class="pain-card__number">01</span>
  <h3>The first order happens. Then the program goes quiet.</h3>
  <p>Your team completes the initial project, but there is no simple buying experience that keeps the client coming back.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="pain-card" data-reveal="">
  <span class="pain-card__number">02</span>
  <h3>Every reorder needs another conversation.</h3>
  <p>Sizes, quantities, addresses, artwork, and payment details are collected manually each time.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="pain-card" data-reveal="">
  <span class="pain-card__number">03</span>
  <h3>A competitor looks more prepared.</h3>
  <p>When a client asks for an online company store, a slow or unclear response can make your business appear less capable.</p>
  </article>
  </div>
  <p class="pain-closing" data-reveal="">The problem is not getting the first order. It is making the <span>next order easy.</span></p>
  </div>
  </section><section class="section outcome-section" id="outcomes">
  <div class="page-shell outcome-layout">
  <div class="outcome-sticky" data-reveal="">
  <p class="eyebrow">What the service is</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">A modern company store built around the client program.</h2>
  <p class="section-copy">Swishtag builds the Shopify company store for you, in your Shopify account or your client's. We load the products, make the logo mockups, and set up payments, shipping, and employee login. You keep the client relationship, the merchandise strategy, and the margin.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="outcome-list">
  <div class="service-definition" data-reveal="">
  <span class="service-definition__label">Company Store</span>
  <h3>Not just a POD website. A repeatable ordering program.</h3>
  <p>Your client gets a branded online store where approved users can browse products, select options, place orders, and reorder. Behind the storefront, products can be produced after the order, shipped from existing inventory, or fulfilled through a combination of both.</p>
  </div>
  <div aria-label="Supported company store fulfillment models" class="fulfillment-grid">
  <article class="fulfillment-card fulfillment-card--pod" data-reveal="">
  <span class="fulfillment-card__number">01</span>
  <h3>Print on demand</h3>
  <p>Products are produced after an order is placed. Best for testing demand, reducing upfront inventory, and supporting apparel sizes or long-tail products.</p>
  <small>Best when demand is uncertain</small>
  </article>
  <article class="fulfillment-card fulfillment-card--stock" data-reveal="">
  <span class="fulfillment-card__number">02</span>
  <h3>Stocked inventory</h3>
  <p>Core products are produced in advance and held for faster fulfillment. Best for predictable demand, better unit economics, kits, and consistent delivery.</p>
  <small>Best for established programs</small>
  </article>
  <article class="fulfillment-card fulfillment-card--hybrid" data-reveal="">
  <span class="fulfillment-card__number">03</span>
  <h3>Hybrid fulfillment</h3>
  <p>Stock the high-volume products and use print on demand or preorders for the rest. This balances speed, margin, product choice, and inventory risk.</p>
  <small>Best for scalable programs</small>
  </article>
  </div>
  <div class="why-shopify-panel" data-reveal="">
  <div class="why-shopify-panel__heading">
  <p class="eyebrow">Why Shopify for company stores</p>
  <h3>Use Shopify when the client opportunity needs more than a basic store template.</h3>
  <p>Shopify makes sense when the distributor needs stronger branding, a fast mobile store, flexible fulfillment, and full control of products, prices, and orders.</p>
  </div>
  <div aria-label="Reasons distributors choose Shopify company stores" class="why-shopify-grid">
  <article><span>01</span><h4>Premium client experience</h4><p>Present a mobile-friendly storefront that feels like the client’s brand rather than a generic internal order form.</p></article>
  <article><span>02</span><h4>Flexible program model</h4><p>Support print on demand, stocked inventory, preorders, or a practical hybrid setup in one commerce experience.</p></article>
  <article><span>03</span><h4>You own the store</h4><p>It's your Shopify account. If you ever stop working with us, the store keeps running and everything stays yours.</p></article>
  <article><span>04</span><h4>Expand without rebuilding</h4><p>Add products anytime, and clone the company store for another client or location for $300, live in 2 to 3 business days.</p></article>
  </div>
  <div class="why-shopify-note"><strong>Our position:</strong> Shopify is not the cheapest choice for every store. It is the right choice when a valuable client program needs a better buying experience and more room to grow.</div>
  </div><div class="why-shopify-panel" data-reveal=""><div class="why-shopify-panel__heading"><p class="eyebrow">Why distributors say yes</p></div><div class="why-shopify-grid">
